Live parallel roots across garbage collection
==============================================

Run ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_parallel"]);'
The appended 21 cases bring that registered file to 94 problems.

The tests use Maxima's public GARBAGE_COLLECT, require its true return value
and independently observe a collection. SBCL's GC epoch changes after a
collection; CCL's GCCOUNTS and CLISP's fourth ROOM value count collections.
ECL's GC-STATS supplies a count and its previous status. The observer
restores that NIL/T/:FULL status on normal return, errors and THROW, without
resetting counters. Only the fixture's observation/collection pair is locked;
mathematics, spawning, joining and nested computations run outside that lock.

These observations cover SBCL, CCL, CLISP and ECL. Other runtimes retain the
ordinary object/arithmetic controls but skip forced-collection assertions.
Threadless configurations run caller/fallback/nested controls. No heap sizes,
collection thresholds, pinning or allocation hooks are changed.

Each corpus contains 16 reproducible roots: cycles, aliased lists/vectors,
EQ hash tables, lexical closures, byte arrays, large integers, Maxima
expressions and bigfloat values. Three collections must preserve the explicit
alias relationships, field contents, exact integer identities and independently
bounded rational approximations. A separate bit-vector snapshot of the signed mantissa, with copied header
flags, precision and exponent, also detects individual bit or precision
changes that could remain inside the numeric error bound. The no-GC controls validate
the same oracle.
Each worker creates its own graph; the controller receives only a boolean,
so its stack cannot accidentally keep the worker's roots alive.

Two native workers either wait with their roots live across three rendezvous
or keep allocating and checking mathematical results while the controller
collects. Progress from both workers is required before and after collection.
Native handles must be distinct, and both jobs must finish without errors.
Setup failures cancel and join already started workers. Rendezvous have
15-second deadlines; active work is also bounded if the controller stalls.

Managed references are the Lisp runtime's responsibility. SBCL/CCL can move
objects; the tested ECL configuration uses Boehm. These tests neither demand
that a particular object moves nor read raw object addresses. They provide
regression evidence for these runtime paths, not certification of every GC,
build, foreign raw pointer or asynchronous interruption mechanism.

Primary references:
https://github.com/sbcl/sbcl/blob/sbcl-2.2.9/src/code/gc.lisp
https://ccl.clozure.com/docs/static/ccl.html
https://clisp.sourceforge.io/impnotes/environment-enq.html
https://ecl.common-lisp.dev/static/files/manual/ecl-21.2.1/Memory-Management.html
ECL21.2.1 src/c/alloc_2.d: SI_GC_STATS returns the previous statistics status.

Known CCL printer limitation (#51)
---------------------------------
The first exact snapshot used WRITE-TO-STRING with *PRINT-READABLY* true.
On unmodified CCL 1.13 LinuxX8664, concurrent CCL:GC can make ordinary
symbols, conses or integers signal PRINT-NOT-READABLE. A standalone
reproducer requiring no Maxima failed in five fresh processes; five controls
without explicit GC passed. The same rejected object prints after GC stops.
See https://github.com/gunterkoenigsmann/maxima-multithreading/issues/51
for the complete reproducer and observations. Its precise cause is unresolved.

The current snapshot uses LOGBITP and a separately allocated bit vector;
it does not invoke the Lisp printer while workers are active. This preserves
the exact bit checks but does not fix or certify concurrent Lisp printing.
No runtime monkeypatch, GC inhibition or weakened mathematical expectation
is part of these tests.
